+# Remote Gzipper protocol, version 1
+
+This protocol carries only `data_decoder.mojom.Gzipper` operations between the
+macOS Chromium browser process and a Debian worker reachable through Tailscale.
+It does not carry Mojo messages or Mojo handles.
+
+The connection is a single ordered byte stream. The browser opens a connection
+per Gzipper request. The worker processes one request, returns one response,
+and closes the connection. This deliberately avoids connection reuse, request
+multiplexing, and cancellation semantics in version 1.
+
+## Transport requirements
+
+- The worker must listen only on a Tailscale address or another explicitly
+  private interface. Do not bind this protocol to a public interface.
+- The browser must reject endpoints without an explicit host and port.
+- Version 1 has no application-layer authentication or encryption. Tailscale
+  provides the encrypted, authenticated network path. A later protocol version
+  may add application authentication without changing version 1 framing.
+- The browser applies a 10-second end-to-end timeout. Timeout, connection, or
+  protocol errors cause a local Data Decoder fallback; they are not returned as
+  successful empty output.
+
+## Frame
+
+All integer fields are unsigned and big-endian. A frame is:
+
+| Field | Bytes | Value |
+| --- | ---: | --- |
+| magic | 4 | ASCII `UCD1` |
+| version | 2 | `1` |
+| message type | 1 | See below |
+| operation | 1 | See below |
+| request ID | 8 | Browser-generated nonzero value |
+| payload length | 8 | Number of payload bytes following the header |
+| payload | variable | Operation input, output, or error text |
+
+The fixed header is 24 bytes. A receiver must read exactly one complete frame;
+extra bytes, a bad magic value, an unknown version, or an invalid field causes
+the request to fail.
+
+The maximum payload length is 64 MiB. The browser rejects larger input before
+connecting. The worker rejects oversized input and output. This bound applies
+to the encoded payload only, not to an eventual local fallback result.
+
+## Request operations
+
+Requests use message type `1`.
+
+| Operation | Value | Maps to |
+| --- | ---: | --- |
+| deflate | 1 | `Gzipper::Deflate(data)` |
+| inflate | 2 | `Gzipper::Inflate(data, max_uncompressed_size)` |
+| compress | 3 | `Gzipper::Compress(data)` |
+| uncompress | 4 | `Gzipper::Uncompress(compressed_data)` |
+
+For operations 1, 3, and 4, the payload is the source bytes. For operation 2,
+the payload is an 8-byte big-endian `max_uncompressed_size` followed by the
+source bytes. `max_uncompressed_size` may be zero only if Chromium permits it;
+the worker must pass it through unchanged to its decompressor limit.
+
+## Responses
+
+A success response uses message type `2`, the original operation, request ID,
+and output bytes as the payload.
+
+A failure response uses message type `3`, the original operation and request
+ID, and a UTF-8 diagnostic payload of at most 1 KiB. The worker must send a
+failure response for malformed input, unsupported operations, size-limit
+violations, and compression failures. It must not send partial output.
+
+The browser treats a failure response as a remote failure and invokes the local
+Gzipper implementation for that request. It must validate that response type,
+operation, and request ID match the pending request before accepting output.
